The idiom pairs
 1. [1, 2, 3].each { |x| out << x.to_s }     "123"
 2. xs.select(&:even?).map { it * it }       [4, 16]
 3. [].empty? / [1].empty?                   true / false
 4. a = [3, 1]; a.sort! returns              [1, 3]  (the receiver, now sorted)
 5. unless [].any? then "empty" end          "empty"
 6. attr_accessor :name; person.name = "ann" "ann"
 7. nil.class / nil.inspect / nil.to_s       NilClass / nil / ""
 8. r = puts "hi"  (printed "hi\n")          r = nil
 9. require "json" twice                     true / false  (loaded once)
10. {a: 1}[:a] / [:z] / fetch(:z, 0)         1 / nil / 0
11. :a.equal?(:a) / "a".equal?("a")          true / false
12. include Greeting; Host.new.hello         "hello from the mixin"  ancestors [Host, Greeting, Object]
13. include Comparable, <=> only: V(1) < V(2) true  between? true
14. include Enumerable, each only: sort / map [1, 2] / [20, 10]
15. method_missing: Finder.new.find_by_name("x") "name=x"
16. Struct.new(:x, :y).new(1, 2)             #<struct Point x=1, y=2>
17. Data.define(:x, :y): with(y: 5) / set @x #<data Coord x=1, y=5> / FrozenError
18. case/in: shape([1, 2]) / shape({name: "ann"}) "pair 1,2" / "named ann"
19. 1/3r + 1/6r                              (1/2)

More than one way: two names, one result
    collect==map true, filter==select true, reduce==inject true, size==length true, yield_self==then true
    Method#== (one definition under two names): then/yield_self true, map/collect false  (two definitions of one behaviour)

The Zen: Ruby ships no equivalent of `import this`
    require "this" -> LoadError: cannot load such file -- this

Word frequency, the same program in both languages
    the      4
    and      2
    barks    2
    dog      2
    fox      2
